slug: lab-reports-for-physiotherapists
title: Reading Lab Reports — Blood Tests That Change Your Treatment
area: Imaging & Reports
level: Clinical
best: Final year onwards
minutes: 13
hook: A blood test can tell you that a patient is anaemic, infected, dehydrated, bleeding or on blood thinners, all of which change what exercise is safe. Learn the common tests and how to use them. || Blood test batata hai ke mareez mein khoon ki kami, infection, paani ki kami, bleeding ya khoon patla karne ki dawai hai, jo sab badalte hain ke kaun si exercise mehfooz hai. Aam tests aur unka istemal seekhein.
goal: By the end, you'll read a basic lab report, know the usual ranges and the meaning of the common tests (blood count, inflammation, sugar, kidney and salts, clotting) and decide when a result limits exercise or needs the doctor. || End tak aap basic lab report parhenge, aam ranges aur aam tests (blood count, sozish, sugar, gurda aur namkiyat, jamne) ka matlab jaanenge aur faisla karenge ke nateeja kab exercise ko mehdood karta hai ya doctor chahiye.

say: A lab report lists each test, the **result**, the **unit** and a **reference range** (the usual range for healthy people in that lab). Results outside the range are **flagged**. A flag is **not a diagnosis**: many results are slightly out of range in healthy people, and the same result means different things in different patients. Physiotherapists use labs to **decide whether it is safe to exercise, to spot warning signs and to talk with the medical team**. Always use the lab's own ranges, and look at the **trend**, not only one result. || Lab report har test, **nateeja**, **unit** aur **reference range** (us lab mein sehatmand logon ki aam range) likhti hai. Range se bahar ke nateeje **flag** hote hain. Flag **tashkhees nahi**: sehatmand logon mein bhi bohat se nateeje thore range se bahar hote hain, aur ek hi nateeja mukhtalif mareezon mein mukhtalif maani rakhta hai. Physiotherapist labs istemal karte hain **yeh tay karne ke liye ke exercise mehfooz hai ya nahi, warning signs pakarne ke liye aur medical team se baat karne ke liye**. Hamesha lab ki apni ranges istemal karein, aur sirf ek nateeje nahi **rujhan** dekhein.
analogy: Think of a lab report as **a car's dashboard**. A warning light tells you something needs checking, but you must read it in context: a low fuel light before a long trip matters more than before a short one. Likewise, a low haemoglobin matters more before heavy exercise than before gentle walking. A good driver checks the whole dashboard and the road. || Lab report ko **gaari ka dashboard** samjhein. Warning light batati hai ke kuch check karna hai, magar usay sahi nazariye se parhna hota hai: lambay safar se pehle tel ki kami chhote safar se zyada ahem hai. Isi tarah halki chahal-qadmi se pehle kam haemoglobin se zyada bhaari exercise se pehle kam haemoglobin ahem hai. Achha driver poora dashboard aur sarak dono dekhta hai.
steps: Using a lab report safely || Lab report ko mehfooz tareeqe se istemal karna
step: Check the basics :: Name, date and time, the lab's reference ranges and the units. Mixing up units (for example mg/dL and mmol/L) is a common error. || Buniyadi cheezein check karein :: Naam, tareekh aur waqt, lab ki reference ranges aur units. Units ka ghalat milna (maslan mg/dL aur mmol/L) aam ghalti hai.
step: Read the key tests :: Blood count (haemoglobin, white cells, platelets), inflammation markers (CRP, ESR), glucose or HbA1c, kidney function and salts (urea, creatinine, eGFR, sodium, potassium), clotting (INR) and others the doctor ordered. || Aham tests parhein :: Blood count (haemoglobin, white cells, platelets), sozish ke markers (CRP, ESR), glucose ya HbA1c, gurde ka function aur namkiyat (urea, creatinine, eGFR, sodium, potassium), jamne ka test (INR) aur doctor ke mangwaye doosre.
step: Look at the trend and the patient :: Compare with earlier results, and with how the patient looks and feels today. A falling haemoglobin matters more than a steady slightly low one. || Rujhan aur mareez dekhein :: Pehle ke nateejon aur aaj mareez kaisa dikhta aur mehsoos karta hai us se muqabla karein. Girta haemoglobin musalsal thore kam haemoglobin se zyada ahem hai.
step: Decide: go, modify or hold :: Use your unit's limits and the medical team's advice to continue, modify or stop exercise, and tell the doctor about worrying results. Record your decision. || Faisla: chalein, badlein ya rokein :: Apni unit ki hadood aur medical team ke mashware se exercise jaari rakhein, badlein ya rokein, aur fikr wale nateejon ke baare mein doctor ko batayein. Apna faisla likhein.

check: Which result raises the concern of anaemia? || Kaun sa nateeja khoon ki kami ki fikr barhata hai?
ok: Low haemoglobin || Kam haemoglobin || Yes — haemoglobin carries oxygen, so low levels can cause tiredness and breathlessness. || Haan — haemoglobin oxygen le jata hai, is liye kam hone par thakan aur saans ki tangi ho sakti hai.
no: High platelet count || Platelets ki zyada tadaad || That is a different issue. || Yeh alag masla hai.
no: Normal sodium || Normal sodium || That is not anaemia. || Yeh khoon ki kami nahi.
compare: Common blood tests and typical adult ranges (your lab's range comes first) || Aam blood tests aur aam bale logon ki ranges (pehle aap ki lab ki range)
head: Feature || Khasiyat ;; Typical range || Aam range ;; Why it matters to a physiotherapist || Physiotherapist ke liye kyun ahem
row: Haemoglobin || Haemoglobin ;; About 13 to 17 g/dL in men, 12 to 15.5 g/dL in women || Mardon mein takreeban 13 se 17 g/dL, khawateen mein 12 se 15.5 g/dL ;; Low: tiredness, breathlessness, a lower exercise limit || Kam: thakan, saans ki tangi, kam exercise ki hadd
row: White cells (WBC) || White cells (WBC) ;; About 4 to 11 x 10^9 per litre || Takreeban 4 se 11 x 10^9 fi litre ;; High: infection or stress; very low: infection risk || Zyada: infection ya dabao; bohat kam: infection ka khatra
row: Platelets || Platelets ;; About 150 to 400 x 10^9 per litre || Takreeban 150 se 400 x 10^9 fi litre ;; Low: bleeding and bruising risk, avoid heavy resistance and contact work || Kam: bleeding aur neel ka khatra, bhaari resistance aur contact kaam se bachein
row: CRP || CRP ;; Under about 5 to 10 mg/L || Takreeban 5 se 10 mg/L se kam ;; High: inflammation or infection; use with the whole picture || Zyada: sozish ya infection; poori tasveer ke saath istemal karein
row: HbA1c || HbA1c ;; Under 5.7 percent normal; 6.5 percent or more suggests diabetes || 5.7 percent se kam normal; 6.5 percent ya zyada diabetes batata hai ;; Poor control raises risk with exercise, wounds and feet || Kamzor qaboo exercise, zakhmon aur pairon mein khatra barhata hai
row: Potassium || Potassium ;; About 3.5 to 5.0 mmol/L || Takreeban 3.5 se 5.0 mmol/L ;; Too high or low can cause dangerous heart rhythms || Bohat zyada ya kam khatarnak dil ki rhythm paida kar sakta hai
row: INR || INR ;; About 1 in people not on warfarin; the target is usually 2 to 3 on warfarin || Warfarin na lene walon mein takreeban 1; warfarin par aam taur par 2 se 3 target ;; High: bleeding risk, avoid falls and heavy manual techniques || Zyada: bleeding ka khatra, girne aur bhaari manual techniques se bachein
memory: **Range, trend, patient.** A single result outside the range is only a prompt. Check the lab's range, look at the trend and look at the patient before you decide. When a result could harm, ask the doctor. || **Range, rujhan, mareez.** Range se bahar ek nateeja sirf ishara hai. Lab ki range, rujhan aur mareez dekh kar faisla karein. Nateeja nuqsan de sakta ho to doctor se poochhein.
mistake: **Quoting a result as a diagnosis, or changing medicines.** A physiotherapist should not diagnose from a lab or advise a change of medicine. Report the finding and its effect on exercise, and let the doctor interpret it and decide on treatment. Respect the patient's privacy when handling results. || **Nateeje ko tashkhees ki tarah batana ya dawaiyan badalna.** Physiotherapist ko lab se tashkhees ya dawai badalne ka mashwara nahi dena chahiye. Finding aur exercise par is ka asar report karein, aur doctor ko tabeer aur ilaj ka faisla karne dein. Nateejon ko sambhalte hue mareez ki raaz daari ka khayal rakhein.
check: A patient's potassium is 6.2 mmol/L. What should you do? || Mareez ka potassium 6.2 mmol/L hai. Aap kya karein?
ok: Do not exercise and tell the medical team at once || Exercise na karwayein aur foran medical team ko batayein || Yes — a high potassium can cause serious heart rhythm problems. || Haan — zyada potassium dil ki rhythm ke sangeen masail paida kar sakta hai.
no: Encourage a longer walk to flush it out || Is ko nikalne ke liye lambi chahal-qadmi karwayein || Exercise can make it worse. || Exercise isay bigaad sakti hai.
no: Advise the patient to stop a medicine || Mareez ko dawai band karne ka kahein || Only the doctor decides on medicines. || Sirf doctor dawaiyon ka faisla karta hai.
check: A patient on warfarin has an INR of 4.5 and wants a deep tissue massage. What is best? || Warfarin lene wale mareez ka INR 4.5 hai aur woh deep tissue malish chahta hai. Behtareen kya hai?
ok: Avoid deep or forceful techniques and tell the medical team about the high INR || Gehri ya zor wali techniques se bachein aur zyada INR ke baare mein medical team ko batayein || Yes — a high INR raises the bleeding risk. || Haan — zyada INR bleeding ka khatra barhata hai.
no: Go ahead; INR does not matter || Karein; INR se farq nahi parta || It can cause serious bruising or bleeding. || Isse sangeen neel ya bleeding ho sakti hai.
no: Tell the patient to take extra vitamin K || Mareez ko zyada vitamin K lene ko kahein || That is a medical decision. || Yeh medical faisla hai.
clinic: **Ask the right question.** Before a session, ask: "Have any new blood tests come back, and is there anything I should know for today's exercise?" Keep a short note of the key results for patients with heart, kidney, diabetes or blood-thinner issues, and update it after each review. || **Sahi sawal poochhein.** Session se pehle poochhein: "Kya koi naye blood tests aaye hain, aur aaj ki exercise ke liye mujhe kuch jaanna chahiye?" Dil, gurde, diabetes ya khoon patla karne ki dawai wale mareezon ke aham nateejon ka mukhtasar note rakhein aur har review ke baad update karein.
reveal: Why should you use the lab's own reference range? || Lab ki apni reference range kyun istemal karni chahiye? || Different labs use different methods, equipment and populations, so ranges and sometimes units differ. A result that is normal in one lab can be flagged in another. The range printed on the report is the right yardstick for that result. || Mukhtalif labs mukhtalif tareeqe, aalat aur aabadi istemal karti hain, is liye ranges aur kabhi units mukhtalif hote hain. Ek lab mein normal nateeja doosri mein flag ho sakta hai. Report par chhapi range us nateeje ka sahi paimana hai.
case: A 72-year-old woman on a cardiac rehabilitation programme becomes more breathless on her usual walk. Her blood test shows haemoglobin 8.5 g/dL (it was 12 two months ago) and she looks pale. || A falling haemoglobin with pallor and increased breathlessness suggests anaemia, possibly from bleeding, and needs medical review before more exercise. || Stop the session, record observations, report the blood result and symptoms to the medical team and hold exercise until they have reviewed her. || The doctor finds a cause and treats it; she resumes rehabilitation once her haemoglobin recovers.
safety: **Report to the doctor straight away** for severe anaemia, a very low platelet count, a very high or low potassium or sodium, very high glucose with unwellness, a high INR with bruising or bleeding, or signs of infection with raised white cells and fever. Do not start or progress exercise on these results without medical clearance. Never advise a change of medicines. || **Foran doctor ko batayein** shadeed anaemia, bohat kam platelets, bohat zyada ya kam potassium ya sodium, beemari ke saath bohat zyada glucose, neel ya bleeding ke saath zyada INR, ya zyada white cells aur bukhar ke saath infection ki nishaniyan. In nateejon par medical ijazat ke baghair exercise shuru ya tez na karein. Dawaiyon ki tabdeeli ka mashwara kabhi na dein.
summary: A lab report gives the result, unit and reference range; a flag is not a diagnosis, and trends and the patient matter more than one number. || Lab report nateeja, unit aur reference range deti hai; flag tashkhees nahi, aur ek number se zyada rujhan aur mareez ahem hain.
summary: Know the key tests: haemoglobin, white cells, platelets, CRP, HbA1c, potassium and INR, and how each changes what exercise or technique is safe. || Aham tests jaanein: haemoglobin, white cells, platelets, CRP, HbA1c, potassium aur INR, aur har ek exercise ya technique ki hifazat kaise badalta hai.
summary: Report worrying results to the doctor, hold exercise when needed and never diagnose or change medicines. || Fikr wale nateeje doctor ko batayein, zaroorat par exercise rokein aur kabhi tashkhees ya dawaiyan na badlein.
card: What are the three parts of a lab result? || Lab nateeje ke teen hissay kya hain? || The result, the unit and the reference range. || Nateeja, unit aur reference range.
card: Which test shows how well diabetes is controlled over months? || Kaun sa test mahino mein diabetes ke qaboo ko dikhata hai? || HbA1c. || HbA1c.
card: Which result raises bleeding risk in people on warfarin? || Warfarin lene walon mein kaun sa nateeja bleeding ka khatra barhata hai? || A high INR. || Zyada INR.
card: Why is a very high or low potassium dangerous? || Bohat zyada ya kam potassium khatarnak kyun hai? || It can cause serious heart rhythm problems. || Yeh dil ki rhythm ke sangeen masail paida kar sakta hai.
deeper: Vital signs skill || /skills/vital-signs || Measure what is happening at the bedside. || Bistar par jo ho raha hai usay naapein.
deeper: Cardiac rehabilitation lesson || /learn/cardiac-rehabilitation-phases || How results affect safe exercise after a heart event. || Nateeje dil ke waqiye ke baad mehfooz exercise par kaise asar daalte hain.
source: Pagana KD, Pagana TJ, Pagana TN. Mosby's Diagnostic and Laboratory Test Reference. 14th ed. St Louis: Elsevier; 2019. || Mosby diagnostic and laboratory test reference
source: Goodman CC, Snyder TEK. Differential Diagnosis for Physical Therapists: Screening for Referral. 6th ed. St Louis: Elsevier; 2018. || differential diagnosis physical therapists screening for referral
